# Rules for Cline in this repo

These apply to EVERY task, on top of whatever the task file says. If a task file contradicts a
rule here, follow this file and say so in your report.

## 1. Git is not yours

- NEVER run `git add`, `git commit`, `git push`, `git checkout`, `git reset`, `git stash`,
  `git clean`, or anything else that moves HEAD or discards work. A human reviews and commits.
- Never create or delete branches, tags, or remotes.
- Never edit `.git/` or any git config.

## 2. Stay inside the file list you were given

- The task names the files you may create or modify. That list is exhaustive. If the work seems
  to need a file that is not on the list, STOP and report it — do not touch it "just this once".
- When a task says "add tests", the module under test is READ-ONLY. A test that fails is a
  finding, not a defect in the test — see rule 4.
- Never touch: `SESSION_LOG.md`, `CLINE_LOG.md`, `INFRASTRUCTURE.md`, `RELEASE_PLAN.md`,
  `BUSINESS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.clinerules`, `ACCOUNTS.md`, `howto.md`, anything in `.github/`,
  any migration under `packages/db/`, or any lockfile. These are owned by the humans and by
  Claude, and a rewrite of them destroys the project's memory.
- Never write a report file. Reports go in your final message only. `CLINE_REPORT.md` is
  obsolete — do not create it.

## 3. Never widen the blast radius

- No new dependencies. Do not edit `package.json`, `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, `tsconfig*.json`,
  `vitest.config.*`, `next.config.*` or `tailwind.config.ts` unless the task names that exact
  file.
- No reformatting of code you were not asked to change. No import reordering, no prettier sweep,
  no renaming for style. A diff full of noise cannot be reviewed and will be rejected whole.
- Serbian UI copy is product text, not strings to improve. Copy it VERBATIM when a test needs it;
  never retype from memory, never "fix" the wording, never translate it.
- Do not delete or weaken an existing test to make something pass. Ever.

## 4. When the code looks wrong, report — do not fix

If a task's expectation and the implementation disagree, you have found either a bug or a wrong
brief, and you cannot tell which from inside the task. So:

- Do NOT silently change the implementation to match the brief.
- Do NOT weaken the test to match the implementation.
- Write the test as the brief describes, let it fail, and say plainly in your report: what fails,
  the concrete scenario in which the code misbehaves, and which of the two you believe is wrong.

This has already happened once and the refusal was the right call — the brief was wrong, not the
code. Refusing costs one short round trip; a quietly weakened test costs a production incident.

## 5. Secrets

- Never print, echo, log, or copy the contents of `.env`, `.env.local`,
  `~/.cline/data/settings/providers.json`, or any API key — not even partially, not even masked.
  Masking by slicing the value has leaked keys in this project twice.
- Never send repository content to any external service beyond your own model API.
- Never start a dev server, never run `next build`, and never call a real provider API. Tests
  must mock the network; a test that opens a socket is a failed task.

## 6. Definition of done means all of it

Before you report success, the task's own gates must have actually run and passed —
typically `pnpm -r typecheck` plus the relevant `pnpm --filter <pkg> test`. Paste the real
pass-count line in your report. Do not report "all checks pass" from memory or intent.

If `git status --short` shows a file you did not touch, say so explicitly rather than claiming a
clean tree — but never `git checkout` it away.

## 7. Your report is audited, not trusted

Every run is verified independently: the diff is read, and for test tasks the implementation is
deliberately broken to confirm the new tests fail for the right reason. Overstating what you did
does not get past that and only costs a round trip. An honest "I could not do step 3, here is
why" is worth more than a confident summary that does not survive the audit.
